import { FilesystemReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> FilesystemReaderCopy = FilesystemReaderCopy {
  summary: "Check a path supplied as text, then obtain a Path for file operations or a string for display.",
  taskTitle: "Display where a report will be saved",
  purpose: "Turn the setting reports/report.txt into a value usable as a path and display it. The std/path module handles path spelling. This example opens no file and creates no reports directory.",
  setup: "import * as paths lets us use public names from std/path as paths.parse. The call paths.parse \"reports/report.txt\" passes one string argument. Its result has two possible shapes: Left carries a problem with the spelling, and Right carries a Path. match chooses the line for that shape. Left reason names the problem reason; Right file names the Path file.",
  after: [
    "The input is valid, so the Right file line prints Saving reports/report.txt. paths.render file turns the Path back into a String. Inside a backtick-delimited string, ${...} inserts the result of the enclosed expression.",
    "pub effect fn main declares the program entry point. println prints a string followed by a newline. On the failure branch, show reason converts the error value into display text. Having both branches means that changing the input to an empty string will display the reason instead.",
    "A Path holds a checked path spelling. It is distinct from a string: pass file from the successful parse branch to a file-reading function. Use render for display or logging; an arbitrary String cannot stand in for a Path."
  ],
  details: [
    "The separator is / regardless of the operating system. Paths distinguish relative spellings such as reports/report.txt, the POSIX root /, Windows roots such as C:/, and share roots such as //server/share/. parse rejects empty text, NUL (the zero character), backslashes, and malformed drive or share roots.",
    "parse keeps . and .. segments, and render returns the stored spelling. Neither case folding nor Unicode normalization happens here. Valid spelling establishes nothing about file existence, read/write permission, or symbolic-link destinations.",
    "Use child to add one filename to a directory. Use std/fs to read file contents. Keeping text validation separate from actual file operations lets you handle invalid input separately from failures during execution."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "TS can also represent a destination as a string. Checking an input and displaying it can already be concise.",
  comparisonAfter: "The useful difference here is that the checked value has type Path rather than String. Later functions can require that checked value, although the type makes no promise about the state of the disk."
}
